Here's how you can create an input field with a clickable SVG search icon (magnifying glass) on the right side that triggers a function when clicked:

### HTML + CSS + JavaScript Solution:

```html
<div class="search-container">
  <input type="text" id="search-input" placeholder="Search...">
  <svg class="search-icon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24">
    <path d="M15.5 14h-.79l-.28-.27a6.5 6.5 0 0 0 1.48-5.34c-.47-2.78-2.79-5-5.59-5.34a6.505 6.505 0 0 0-7.27 7.27c.34 2.8 2.56 5.12 5.34 5.59a6.5 6.5 0 0 0 5.34-1.48l.27.28v.79l4.25 4.25c.41.41 1.08.41 1.49 0 .41-.41.41-1.08 0-1.49L15.5 14zm-6 0C7.01 14 5 11.99 5 9.5S7.01 5 9.5 5 14 7.01 14 9.5 11.99 14 9.5 14z"/>
  </svg>
</div>

<style>
  .search-container {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-block;
    width: 300px; /* Adjust as needed */
  }

  #search-input {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 10px 40px 10px 15px; /* Right padding for icon */
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 16px;
  }

  .search-icon {
    position: absolute;
    right: 10px;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    cursor: pointer;
    fill: #555;
  }

  .search-icon:hover {
    fill: #000;
  }
</style>

<script>
  document.querySelector('.search-icon').addEventListener('click', function() {
    const searchTerm = document.getElementById('search-input').value;
    console.log('Searching for:', searchTerm);
    // Call your function here with the search term
    yourSearchFunction(searchTerm);
  });

  function yourSearchFunction(term) {
    // Your search logic here
    alert('Searching for: ' + term);
  }
</script>
```

### React Solution (if you're using React):

```jsx
import { useState } from 'react';

function SearchInput() {
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('');

  const handleSearch = () => {
    console.log('Searching for:', searchTerm);
    // Call your search function here
    yourSearchFunction(searchTerm);
  };

  const handleKeyPress = (e) => {
    if (e.key === 'Enter') {
      handleSearch();
    }
  };

  return (
    <div style={{ position: 'relative', display: 'inline-block', width: '300px' }}>
      <input
        type="text"
        value={searchTerm}
        onChange={(e) => setSearchTerm(e.target.value)}
        onKeyPress={handleKeyPress}
        placeholder="Search..."
        style={{
          width: '100%',
          padding: '10px 40px 10px 15px',
          border: '1px solid #ccc',
          borderRadius: '4px',
          fontSize: '16px'
        }}
      />
      <svg
        onClick={handleSearch}
        style={{
          position: 'absolute',
          right: '10px',
          top: '50%',
          transform: 'translateY(-50%)',
          cursor: 'pointer',
          fill: '#555'
        }}
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        width="24"
        height="24"
      >
        <path d="M15.5 14h-.79l-.28-.27a6.5 6.5 0 0 0 1.48-5.34c-.47-2.78-2.79-5-5.59-5.34a6.505 6.505 0 0 0-7.27 7.27c.34 2.8 2.56 5.12 5.34 5.59a6.5 6.5 0 0 0 5.34-1.48l.27.28v.79l4.25 4.25c.41.41 1.08.41 1.49 0 .41-.41.41-1.08 0-1.49L15.5 14zm-6 0C7.01 14 5 11.99 5 9.5S7.01 5 9.5 5 14 7.01 14 9.5 11.99 14 9.5 14z"/>
      </svg>
    </div>
  );
}

function yourSearchFunction(term) {
  // Your search logic here
  console.log('Searching for:', term);
}
```

### Key Features:
1. The SVG icon is positioned absolutely on the right side of the input
2. The icon is clickable and triggers a function when clicked
3. The input has proper padding to prevent text from being hidden behind the icon
4. The icon changes color on hover for better UX
5. The solution includes both vanilla JS and React versions

You can adjust the width, colors, and other styling properties to match your design system.

